/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au & =-3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/44ce64acad55425e3d0056653ac06cb3fb37d691a425b1ef32e69b29a47074be.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-75733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/19d7f2ee9427618ca71d9fba647193d60e9918aeb0d98f75a70026b5d8ba2212.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/3a254a9edb04962e18a36da534f7c844c7e8ce105efd88abb5c16c7e5829c0ce.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/44ce64acad55425e3d0056653ac06cb3fb37d691a425b1ef32e69b29a47074be.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/8aa05de3863b1b4f840ee45b2ddc91ea2273de645a5add2fe127b1499e4a641f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/cb12b574f67ada2b2a3a1249e0b760c7e30bf474bf7b8147dc07998757440fba.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/44ce64acad55425e3d0056653ac06cb3fb37d691a425b1ef32e69b29a47074be.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k-1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =(k-1)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4k-4 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16=4k-4, \\\\ -4k+4=-4k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-12=4k\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nThe second equation is true for any value of $k.$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(5k-4)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6k=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-75610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140=6c \\\\ -7c+154=84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-18233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107837" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-4c=-3 \\\\ -2+8c=6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-106946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I,II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-107137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-7.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-7v)=-7(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-7(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=3v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=3{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{3}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v)=5Av=5(-3v)=-3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=-3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-36678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $5$ then $10v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $5.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $6.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b7ecfca31c0a02c61ffa14aeee33bc80637047bb29b2e10ed4ba4c0d4a20429c"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "q-37090" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d1fa34c97a4729667f7b1d6e02285ec9d007bbdb486ea09c69045740fc58421"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "37718f8c56f156afc6205eadb8b0216ae57b18e7cf10b62741ec05741a79309e"} {"question" "e-5476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5476.json" "file_sha256" "37718f8c56f156afc6205eadb8b0216ae57b18e7cf10b62741ec05741a79309e"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "08cf588916e0abecbc72c5e54322b6e437913814079c9399ea0086d8c4cb9f20"} {"question" "e-5891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bf65510b8cef0e0162e3054bcf31405ac4e4268a759f6493a06c9f0314c1d482.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5891.json" "file_sha256" "08cf588916e0abecbc72c5e54322b6e437913814079c9399ea0086d8c4cb9f20"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/22ef70dcb0f7d3cba1c9d5514b40800144eb97edd004041ebb3516062a9e3e48.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/d1c2b9c36ec942a1c4113f1ff3a6221028f36c6ac4d109351ebab6bb48f358c4.png) Determine the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "2cfb09c16dc98bd056192d5c637cfc48223d6f9b54d53df1512fe9852d3f7752"} {"question" "e-5478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5478.json" "file_sha256" "2cfb09c16dc98bd056192d5c637cfc48223d6f9b54d53df1512fe9852d3f7752"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/defb480233b4e48f90cb07fef771f2fdb62b6d7d5cb116393d74518eb3b37db8.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bb62b77da84018c07d4f3dfefc297684974db78b326bd0736469dc83572ae5b6.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "e3af67a936680cfb49e6e601fea2f4fb0d5c4c54df27e928c8cc395f4693bc25"} {"question" "e-5477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/example-5477.json" "file_sha256" "e3af67a936680cfb49e6e601fea2f4fb0d5c4c54df27e928c8cc395f4693bc25"}] "usage" {"q-36791" [] "e-5891" [] "e-5478" [] "q-37087" [] "e-5476" [] "e-5477" []} "basis" 1830 "retractions" [[17592186106225 :question/problem "Determine if $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186106227 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$.\n2. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $λ =-7$.\n3. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$."] [17592186105543 :question/problem "Determine if $1; 1$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186105543 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4$."] [17592186105551 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2$. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/204140201746adea135bd375366ced72.png)"] [17592186105551 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$. So,\n$Av = 2v$.\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/ecd22d908f7242bad08169a51db3796d.png)"] [17592186105545 :question/problem "An eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is $1; 2c$ Determine the value of $c$."] [17592186105545 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAv &= λv \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} &= 6c\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 + 2c \\\\ 1 + 4c\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6c \\\\ 12c^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2 + 2c = 6c; 1 + 4c = 12c^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 4c &= 12c^{2} \\\\\n1 + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} &= 12(\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n3 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n."] [17592186105544 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2$.\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0$.\n3. If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5$, then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5$."] [17592186105544 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n$Av|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}; = 2v$.\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v}$, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2$.\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n- Statement III is true. We have\n$A(2v) = 2Av = 2(5v) = 5(2v)$.\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5(2 \\mathbf{v})$, and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186103860 17592186103861 17592186103862 17592186103863 17592186103864 17592186103872 17592186103873 17592186103874 17592186103875 17592186103876] "immutable_fields" [[17592186103859 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103859 :answer-field/id] [17592186103859 :answer-field/key] [17592186103859 :answer-field/type] [17592186103871 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103871 :answer-field/id] [17592186103871 :answer-field/key] [17592186103871 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
